parts.text:
The `name` prop is used to build the v0 URL (`https://prsui.pr5.dev/r/${name}.json`) and the component source. So `name` must match the registry item name. Good.

Now let me build. Let me set up a todo list and start writing the components.

Let me write the Counter component first.

For counter, I want a polished, springy count-up. Design decisions:
- Default trigger "inView" with `once: true`
- Support `from`, `value`, `duration`, `delay`, `decimals`, `prefix`, `suffix`, `separator`, `decimalSeparator`
- Use `useMotionValue` + `animate()` from motion/react with tween + EASE_OUT
- Render sr-only final value + aria-hidden formatted live value

Let me write it.

```tsx
"use client";

import type { Transition } from "motion/react";
import { animate, useInView, useMotionValue, useMotionValueEvent } from "motion/react";
import { useEffect, useRef, useState } from "react";

import { cn } from "@/lib/utils";

interface CounterProps {
  value: number;
  from?: number;
  duration?: number;
  delay?: number;
  decimals?: number;
  prefix?: string;
  suffix?: string;
  separator?: string;
  decimalSeparator?: string;
  transition?: Transition;
  trigger?: "inView" | "auto";
  className?: string;
  onComplete?: () => void;
}

const Counter = ({
  value,
  from = 0,
  duration = 2,
  delay = 0,
  decimals = 0,
  prefix = "",
  suffix = "",
  separator = ",",
  decimalSeparator = ".",
  transition,
  trigger = "inView",
  className,
  onComplete,
  ...props
}: CounterProps & React.HTMLAttributes<HTMLSpanElement>) => {
  const ref = useRef<HTMLSpanElement>(null);
  const inView = useInView(ref, { once: true, amount: 0.5 });
  const motionValue = useMotionValue(from);
  const [displayValue, setDisplayValue] = useState(from);

  useMotionValueEvent(motionValue, "change", (latest) => {
    setDisplayValue(latest);
  });

  useEffect(() => {
    if (trigger === "inView" && !inView) {
      return;
    }

    const controls = animate(motionValue, value, {
      ...transition,
      duration: transition?.duration ?? duration,
      delay,
      ease: transition?.ease ?? [0.16, 1, 0.3, 1],
      onComplete,
    });

    return () => controls.stop();
  }, [value, from, duration, delay, inView, trigger, transition, motionValue, onComplete]);

  const formatValue = (num: number) => {
    const fixed = num.toFixed(decimals);
    const [int, dec] = fixed.split(".");
    const formattedInt = int.replace(/\B(?=(\d{3})+(?!\d))/g, separator);
    return dec !== undefined ? `${formattedInt}${decimalSeparator}${dec}` : formattedInt;
  };

  return (
    <span ref={ref} className={cn("tabular-nums", className)} {...props}>
      <span className="sr-only">{`${prefix}${formatValue(value)}${suffix}`}</span>
      <span aria-hidden="true">
        {prefix}
        {formatValue(displayValue)}
        {suffix}
      </span>
    </span>
  );
};

export default Counter;
```

Hmm — `useMotionValue` from "motion/react". `useMotionValueEvent` also from "motion/react". Good.

One consideration: when the value prop changes later (e.g., data updates), the effect re-runs and animates to the new value. That's nice behavior. But `from` default is 0 — if value changes from 100 to 200, `from` is still 0 so it'd reset to 0. That's acceptable default. Could allow it. Keep simple.

Also the `transition` prop overriding duration — I combined. Fine.

Now Morphing Text. Design: AnimatePresence + words cycling with blur+y fade. Props: words, interval, className, as, transition per-phase maybe.

```tsx
"use client";

import type { Variants } from "motion/react";
import { AnimatePresence, motion } from "motion/react";
import type { ElementType } from "react";
import { useEffect, useMemo, useState } from "react";

import { cn } from "@/lib/utils";

interface MorphingTextProps {
  words: string[];
  interval?: number;
  className?: string;
  as?: ElementType;
  transition?: {
    enter?: Variants;
    exit?: Variants;
  };
}
```

Actually keep the variants internal and allow simple duration control. Let me keep: words, interval (default 3000), className, as (default "div"). Internal variants with EASE. Optionally `pauseOnHover`.

Let me include `pauseOnHover` for polish (matches logo-marquee's hover pause style). Hmm, but keeping props surface focused is good for registry docs. I'll add pauseOnHover = false default.

```tsx
const enterVariants: Variants = {
  initial: { y: 12, opacity: 0, filter: "blur(12px)" },
  animate: {
    y: 0,
    opacity: 1,
    filter: "blur(0px)",
    transition: { duration: 0.5, ease: [0.16, 1, 0.3, 1] },
  },
};

const exitVariants: Variants = {
  exit: {
    y: -12,
    opacity: 0,
    filter: "blur(12px)",
    transition: { duration: 0.35, ease: [0.32, 0, 0.55, 1] },
  },
};
```

Component:
```tsx
const MorphingText = ({
  words,
  interval = 3000,
  className,
  as: Tag = "div",
  pauseOnHover = false,
  ...props
}: MorphingTextProps & React.HTMLAttributes<HTMLElement>) => {
  const [index, setIndex] = useState(0);
  const [paused, setPaused] = useState(false);

  useEffect(() => {
    if (paused) return;
    if (words.length <= 1) return;
    const id = setInterval(() => {
      setIndex((prev) => (prev + 1) % words.length);
    }, interval);
    return () => clearInterval(id);
  }, [interval, paused, words.length]);

  return (
    <Tag
      className={cn("inline-flex overflow-hidden", className)}
      onMouseEnter={() => pauseOnHover && setPaused(true)}
      onMouseLeave={() => pauseOnHover && setPaused(false)}
      {...props}
    >
      <AnimatePresence mode="wait" initial={false}>
        <motion.span
          key={words[index]}
          variants={enterVariants}
          initial="initial"
          animate="animate"
          exit={exitVariants}
          className="inline-block"
        >
          {words[index]}
        </motion.span>
      </AnimatePresence>
    </Tag>
  );
};
```

Wait, `exit` needs to be passed as prop (exit variant from exitVariants). I pass `exit={exitVariants.exit}`? Actually with motion, you can pass `exit` as a prop, or define it in variants object with an "exit" key. Let me structure variants with enter and exit keys in one object:

```tsx
const variants: Variants = {
  initial: { y: 12, opacity: 0, filter: "blur(12px)" },
  animate: {
    y: 0, opacity: 1, filter: "blur(0px)",
    transition: { duration: 0.5, ease: EASE_OUT },
  },
  exit: {
    y: -12, opacity: 0, filter: "blur(12px)",
    transition: { duration: 0.3, ease: EASE_IN_OUT },
  },
};
```

Then `<motion.span variants={variants} initial="initial" animate="animate" exit="exit">`. Good.

Should I import from lib/ease? For the registry item, if I use `@/lib/ease`, the files list must include `lib/ease.ts`. The counter and morphing-text would both need it. That's fine — include it in the files array. But to keep the component simpler and self-contained (registry items sometimes are standalone), I could inline the ease values. Given existing components like accordion and tooltip include lib/ease.ts in their file lists, it's a well-established pattern. But to minimize dependencies, I'll inline the easing arrays as literals in both. Actually consistency: text-highlight, typewriter, scramble use inline transitions. Let me inline. Simpler and self-contained.

Now let me check `cn` import path: `@/lib/utils`. Yes, used in typewriter.

For demos:
- counter-demo.tsx: a nice demo with a few counters with prefix/suffix/decimals. Maybe a grid of metrics. Use trigger "auto" so it animates immediately? Actually in-view is nicer. But within the preview, inView works since it's in the viewport. Let me use default inView.

Demo for counter: show $ prefix, %, comma-separated large number, decimals.

- morphing-text-demo.tsx: a headline "Design, engineer, ship" morphing.

Docs mdx files follow the scramble-in.mdx pattern.

Now, meta.json add "counter" and "morphing-text" to pages array.

Let me also check: registry.json entries. Follow existing pattern. For counter deps: ["motion"]. For morphing-text: ["motion"].

Note: In `registry.json`, the entries reference `registry/new-york/counter.tsx` target `components/prsui/counter.tsx`.

Let me verify the naming: registry item names are kebab-case: "smooth-input", "split-action", etc. So "counter" and "morphing-text". Title: "Counter" / "Morphing Text".

Let me check the registry.json description wording style. Fine.

Now let me also check how `registry.json` is ordered and whether `public/r` build artifacts need to be regenerated. `pnpm registry:build` generates them. I'll run it after.

Let me write everything now. Let me create the todo list first.

